    "q-66482": {
      "dom_id": "step-q66482",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "Given that $f(x)={x}^{2}+2,$ on which of the following intervals is $f(x)$ invertible?\n\nI. $x\\in (-2,2)$\n\nII. $x\\in [0,\\infty )$\n\nIII. $x\\in (-\\infty ,0]$",
      "worked_solution": "The graph of the function $f(x)={x}^{2}+2$ can be obtained in the following way:\n\n1\\. Take the curve $y={x}^{2}.$\n\n2\\. Translate it by $2$ units up to get $f(x)={x}^{2}+2.$\n\n  ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14243592/assets/ff3d1ac7500d83574b8671b23e99d4a0f1dbd6ab9cebc906aa06ff2c21f95345.png)\n\n  A function is invertible on a particular interval if and only if it's a one-to-one function on that interval. To determine the intervals on which the function is invertible, we use the horizontal line test.\n\n3\\. The function $f(x)$ is not invertible on the interval $x\\in (-2,2).$ On this interval, some horizontal lines will intersect the curve more than once.\n\n  ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14243592/assets/a71ce036adc5dc0b15fce9e367a0c8512699dc6a7814f4e9a1886696d2abad6e.png)\n\n4\\. The function $f(x)$ is invertible on the intervals $x\\in [0,\\infty )$ and  $x\\in (-\\infty ,0].$ On these intervals, any horizontal line will intersect the curve at most once, no matter where we draw the line.\n\n  ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14243592/assets/6858bd1fab8d9659c84fa941cd63ee9f509c8364dce0e753706c640ecfe6e9ec.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only\".",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
